{"step_index":814,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-28T22:15:32Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nThe compiler is emitting real ARM64 — svc #0x80, adrp/add page relocations, proper frame prologue and epilogue. That's not a stub. The taint lattice is doing its job at the AST level: ungated candidate to decision is a hard compile error, not a runtime check. That's the right place for it.\n\nBut here's the thing that should make you pause. The emitted code is a template, not a compiler. Look at what it actually generates: four mov instructions loading constants, two strs, one sys_write, one ret. The hypervector gets a pointer. The ring buffer gets a base address. The taint tags get written as immediates. None of that is computed — it's all literal values the compiler knows at emit time.\n\nA real compiler would lower expressions. x + y becomes an add. A function call becomes a bl. A loop becomes a branch with a condition. Your .sovra source has symbols, but the codegen path doesn't appear to lower any operations on those symbols — it just materializes their metadata as constants.\n\nThat's fine for a v0.1 proof of the pipeline. It's not a language yet. The next milestone isn't more tests passing — it's emitting an add, a bl, a conditional branch from actual .sovra source that contains those operations. Until the compiler lowers control flow and arithmetic, sovrac is a pretty printer with a security linter bolted on.\n\nThe 297-tick arena is still the strongest number in the whole stack.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-28T18:15:32-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
